@section(Introduction)

This document is meant to serve as a guide to maintaining and modifying
the CUCCA VM Network Mailer.  In addition to program logic descriptions,
guidelines are provided for writing @i<Mailer> user exits.  The version
of @i<Mailer> described is experimental version 1.0 (x1.0).

@section(Environment)

@i<Mailer> was developed under CMS/BSEPP version 6 PLC 17.  It has run
under VM/SP with no need to be reassembled.  The following areas may
cause trouble if an incompatible release of VM/CMS comes out:
@begin(description)
DMSSCANN@\The CMS tokenizer.  The NUCON symbol ASCANN is used to obtain
access to this routine and the DMSKEY macro is used to get into system
key. @index(DMSSCANN)

LOCCNT@\Used by the CMS loader.  This NUCON value is used in conjunction
with an SVC 202 CMS LOAD command using the ORIGIN option. @index(LOCCNT)

SFBLOK@\CP Spool file blocks are referenced using the SFBLOK dsect
described in member SPOOL of DMKMAC MACLIB.

VMCMHDR@\VMCF dsects from DMKMAC MACLIB are used for SMSG support.

RSCS tag@\The text of the tag generated by RSCS/Networking is assumed
to have a fixed offset at which point the origin node name and userid
are described.  This offset is described in the RSCSTAG dsect
in MAILER MACLIB.
@end(Description)

@i<Mailer> is designed to operate as a CMS disconnected virtual machine.
It normally does not require any attention by operations personnel.
The VM userid for @i<Mailer> should be configured in the following manner:
@begin(description)
VM Size@\A minimum of 512K is suggested.

VM Userid@\Something self-descriptive such as "Mailer" or "VMMail" is
recommended although not required.  Remote users will receive messages
from this userid when they send mail to users on your system.   The userid, 
once established,  should not be changed without first informing the
person supporting @i<Mailer> for your network since all @i<Mailers> in 
the network maintain a table of the names of other @i<Mailers>.

191 Minidisk@\A writeable CMS minidisk is required for @i<Mailer> use.
Additionally the same or another minidisk is required to store the
@i<Mailer> modules and parameter file.  It is recommend that this
be a seperate read-only disk that your site's maintainer has write 
access to.  This eases maintenance but is not required.

Priviledge Classes@\Class G priviledges are all that are required,  although
the addition of class D priviledges will allow for the use of MSGNOH, 
leading to more readable messages from @i<Mailer>.  
@end(description)


@section(Generation)

@i<Mailer> is written in 370 Assembler.  @i<Mailer> may be reassembled
by using the MAILGEN @index(MAILGEN)CMS exec.
The needed Global Maclibs  (for BSEPP) are: @index(MACLIB)
MAILER, CMSBSE,  CMSLIB,  OSMACRO,  DMKMAC.  Make sure the DMKMAC
MACLIB is last in the search order since it contains a CALL macro that
is incompatible with the OS CALL macro found in OSMACRO.

Once assembled, @i<Mailer> may be loaded and gen'd via:
@begin(example)
LOAD MAILER 

GENMOD MAILER
@end(example)

@section(Operation)

To run @i<Mailer>,  the MAILER TXTLIB @Index(TXTLIB)
must be global'd or the TEXT files
for the user exits must be available.  Simply type "Mailer" and it
will start.  It is suggested that this procedure be put in a CMS PROFILE
EXEC.  Then @i<Mailer> may be started by issuing the CP AUTOLOG command
from the operator console or a service disconnected machine such as
VMUTIL.

@I<Mailer> reads an initialization file whose default name is MAILER
PROFILE.  If this file is found and processed properly,  then @i<Mailer>
will become operational.  If not,  then error messages are printed and
@i<Mailer> will exit.

@section(Data structures - MAILBLOK)

@index(MAILBLOK)
@i<Mailer> communicates with all it's routines almost exclusively
through the MAILBLOK data area.  This central data area is gernerated and
referenced via the MAILBLOK macro in MAILER MACLIB.  The following sections
describe some of the key fields of the MAILBLOK.

@subsection(Stack pointers)

Most routines allocate storage from a common stack.  A stack pointer
is a doubleword containing the next free address in the first word and
the remaining length in the second word.  Other buffers also sometimes
use this method of description.  The stack pointers are:

@begin(description)

STACKORG@\@index(STACKORG)Stack origin.  This is the
beginning address of the stack allocated via GETMAIN in MAILER.

STACK@\@index(STACK)Variable stack start.  The area
between STACKORG and STACK contains static information that is set
once during initialization.  This consists of tables from the Mailer
Profile and the dynamically loaded user exit routines.

STACKP@\@index(STACKP)Current variable stack
pointer.  As each mail file is processed, stack storage is allocated
using STACKP. STACKP generally tends to grow (i.e. more stack space is
used) without ever shrinking.  The order of processing usually
precludes freeing any stack space until all work is completed.  At
this point, STACKP is reset to the value in STACK (in MAILER).
@end(description)

@subsection(MAILHBUF, MAILHREC, MAILHLIN)

MAILHBUF @index(MAILHBUF) is a pointer to the buffer
(allocated from the stack) which contains the text of the message
header as read in from the mail file.  Trailing blanks are trimmed and
X'15' (newline) characters used to delimit records.

MAILHREC @index(MAILHREC) contains the number of
physical records in the header (i.e. how many cards read in).
MAILHLIN contains the count of logical records in the header. This
number is usually the same but may be greater if X'15' characters were
imbedded in a physical record.



@subsection(MAILPTAB)
@index(MAILPTAB)
This is the parameter table.  Entries of the form <keyword>=<value> may
be specified in the Mailer Profile file.  An entry is created in the
MAILPTAB TBLUK table (TBLUK described below) with the keyword as  the
key and the value character string as the value.  Later calls to TBLUK
can be used to find both keywords recognized as @i<Mailer> keywords and
user-defined keywords for use by the user exits.

@subsection(MAILRTAB)
@index(MAILRTAB)
This is the Outgoing table.  The key used is the alias or host name
and the value for each entry is a pointer to a ROUTD dsect (described
below).

@subsection(MAILSTAB)
@index(MAILSTAB)
The special handling table is used for userids that receive special
treatment (i.e. SYSTEM, etc.)  The key for each entry is the userid
and the value is a pointer to a ROUTD.

@subsection(MAILVTAB)
@index(MAILVTAB)
The incoming table is used for validity checking of mail from other
network mailers.  The key consists of the concatenation of the 
RSCS node name (padded to 8 characters) and the VM userid of the
mailer userid at the remote site.  Note that this organization 
allows one site to have multiple mailers (useful since an RSCS
RJE station looks like a userid).  The value for each entry is a pointer
to a VALD dsect described below.

@subsection(MAILKTAB)
@index<MAILKTAB>
The keyword table is built as each mail file is parsed.  The key
to each entry is the field name (i.e. "from", "to", "cc", etc.)
and the value is a pointer to a field descriptor (FIELDD) dsect.

@subsection(MAILFLD, MAILSFLD, MAILCMDL)
@index(MAILFLD)
@index(MAILSFLD)
@index(MAILCMDL)
Pointers to various lists are maintained in MAILBLOK.  MAILFLD 
points to the list of FIELDD's that describe the message header.
MAILSFLD points to the FIELDD which describes the sender of the
mail file.  MAILCMDL points to a list of commands to be 
executed by MAILCMD2.  The list is built by MAILCMD1.

@subsection(MAILSFB, MAILFILE, MAILFTXT)
@index(MAILSFB)
@index(MAILFILE)
@index(MAILFTXT)
The SFBLOK for the current reader file is in MAILSFB.  MAILFILE is
another copy of the file number and MAILFTXT is the character version
of the file number.

@subsection(MAILOPTS - options flags)
@index(MAILOPTS)
The MAILOPTS flags are set from values in the Mailer Profile as well as
by commands.  Flags and their meanings are:
@begin(description)

MOPTDEF@\@index(MOPTDEF)Default values for omitted "date:"
and "from:" fields from local users should be supplied (NYI).

MOPTAUN@\@index(MOPTAUN)Mail from and to unknown hosts
(not in MAILVTAB, or MAILRTAB) is allowed if this flag is on. This is
useful in a network envirnonment where the RSCS tables have been
updated prior to updates to the Mailer Profile.  @end(description)

@subsection(MAILCMD - command flags)
@index(MAILCMD)
The flags in MAILCMD describe commands for Mailer to execute.  These are:
@begin(description)
MCMDRELD@\@index(MCMDRELD)Reload the Mailer.

MCMDHALT@\@index(MCMDHALT)Terminate processing.

MCMDCMD2@\@index(MCMDCMD2)Some commands are queued on the
chain pointed to by MAILCMDL.  Call MAILCMD2@index(MAILCMD2) to process them.
@end(description)


@subsection(MAILSTAT - status flags)
@index(MAILSTAT)
The MAILSTAT flags reflect the current execution status of @i<Mailer>.
These are:
@begin(description)

MSTINWT@\@index(MSTINWT)@i<Mailer> is currently is wait
state for a console or reader interrupt.

MSTQSC@\@index(MSTQSC)@i<Mailer> is currently quiescant.
This means that reader interrupts are accepted but no processing of
files is performed.  All other functions are still active.
@end(description)

@subsection(MAILFLG1 - file flags)
@index(MAILFLG1)
The MAILFLG1 flags describe the status of a particular mail file.  These
are:
@begin(description)
MFLG1LCL@\@index(MFLG1LCL)File is from a local user.

MFLG1GTW@\@index(MFLG1GTW)File is from a gateway interface.

MFLG1EDI@\@index(MFLG1EDI)Message header has been edited to
appear differently from original.

MFLG1REJ@\@index(MFLG1REJ)File has been returned by RSCS (rejected).

MFLG1HDS@\@index(MFLG1HDS)The message header has been
successfully scanned.
@end(description)

@subsection(Other variables)
@begin(description)
CPUSTR@\@index(CPUSTR)RSCS node name of this system.

RSCSNAME@\@index(RSCSNAME)VM userid of RSCS/Networking server.

MAILVMID@\@index(MAILVMID)VM userid of the Mailer. Obtained at
initialization via CP diagnose.

MAINTID@\@index(MAINTID)VM userid of the maintainer.

MAINTNOD@\@index(MAINTNOD)RSCS node name for the maintainer.

MAILNODE@\@index(MAILNODE)Mail file origin node name extracted from
the RSCS tag if not local.

MAILUSER@\@index(MAILUSER)Mail file origin userid.

MAILRTFN@\@Index(MAILRTFN)Mailer Profile file name.

MAILULFN@\@index(MAILULFN)Temporary error log file name.
@end(description)

@section(Data structures - Other Control Blocks)

Several DSECTS are described in the BLOCKS macro of MAILER
MACLIB.  A brief description of the fields of each follows:

@subsection(TOKEND - Token Descriptor)
Character strings read by @i<Mailer> are tokenized prior to
parsing.  These tokens are described by the TOKEND@index(TOKEND)
dsect.  Fields in a TOKEND are:
@begin(description)
TOKSTR@\Address of character string described by the token.

TOKLEN@\Length of the string.

TOKNEXT@\Address of next TOKEND in order of appearance.

TOKCODE@\Code for type of token.  Types are: ATOM, QUOTED STRING,
LEFT BRACKET,  RIGHT BRACKET, ATSIGN, COMMA, COMMENT.
@end(description)

The use of TOKENDs makes editing the message header (when necessary)
easy to do.  A new TOKEND can be created with it's string located
in storage seperate from the current string of tokens.  It can
then be inserted into the token list by simply modifying two TOKNEXT
pointers.  This is normally not done,  but might become necessary
when processing host lists or when a file has to be edited to
conform to another mail system.

@subsection(ADADDR and ADNODE - Address descriptors)

@index(ADADDR)@index(ADNODE)
The ADADDR and ADNODE dsects are used in the representation of
addresses and address lists.  An address is described by a tree
structure with the ADADDR at the root.  Address lists consist of
linked lists of the ADADDR trees.  See figure @ref(ADPFIG) for
an example.  Fields in the ADADDR are:
@begin(description)
ADANEXT@\Address of next ADADDR record.

ADAUCNT@\Number of ADNODEs used in the description of the user name.

ADAULST@\Address of first ADNODE in the user name list.

ADAHCNT@\Number of ADNODEs used in the description of the host path.

ADAHLST@\Address of first ADNODE in the host path list.
@end(description)

@subsection(FIELDD - Field descriptor)
FIELDD blocks@index(FIELDD) are used to describe a message header
field. 
@begin(description)
FINEXT@\Address of next FIELDD in order of apperance.

FIEXTN@\Address of next FIELDD that is an extension (i.e. has the
same field name).

FIFLG@\Flags:
@begin(description)
FINVFLG@\(Set by the field parser) Indicates that the field is
"invisible".  That is, it should not be output when the mail file
is sent.  This is used to hide password fields and other inter-mailer
communication information that the user shouldn't see.

FIEXTFLG@\Indicates an extension FIELDD.
@end(description)

FISTR@\Beginning address of field string.  Points just after the
colon that follows the field name.

FIEND@\End address of field string.

FITOK@\Pointer to token list.  The first token is the field name.

FIPARS@\Pointer to parse list.  Optionally used by some field parsers.
@end(description)

@subsection(ACTADR - Block for address requiring action)
ACTADR blocks @index(ACTADR) are used to maintain a list
of the Outgoing ROUTDs and ADADDRs that require action (i.e. this
@i<Mailer> is a server for).  Fields are:
@begin(description)
ACTNEXT@\Address of next ACTADR.

ACTADA@\Address of ADADDR record for the address requiring action.

ACTRT@\Address of ROUTD for the host named in the address.
@end(description)


@begin(Figure)
@caption(Representation of an address)
@tag(ADPFIG)
@begin(example,group)

                    (FIELDD fields)
FISTR FITOK             FIPARS                 FIEND
:    /                     |                       :
:   /                    ADADDR                    :
:  /                     |   |                     :
: /                 ADNODE   ADNODE                :
:/                    |        |                   :
:T-->T------>T---->T->T-->T--->T->T------->T       :
:|   |       |     |  |   |    |  |        |       :
To: Alan Crosswell <EACUS at CUVMB> (Mailer example)

                      (T=TOKEND)
@end(example)
@end(Figure)

@section(Modules)

This section describes the function of each module in some detail.
Further specifics may be found in the source to the named module.

@subsection(Standard subroutine linkage)

@index(Subroutine linkage)
OS standard linkage conventions are used.  In most cases,  the OS CALL
macro is used.  Unless otherwise noted,  the modules described below
are called with a single argument:  the MAILBLOK address.  The following
return codes interpretations are used except for some special cases:
@index(Subroutine return code)
@begin(description)
0@\No error.  This module has completed succesfully.

4@\Minor error.  For example, the user specified something wrong in his mail 
file and it should be returned.

8@\??? I don't remember.

12@\Internal error caught.  Probably due to a stack overflow or some other
detectable but not immediately correctable error.

16@\Unknown error.  
@end(description)

@subsection(MAILER - Main program)

@Index(MAILER)
@ux<Called By> CMS.

@ux<Calls> READINIT, SETINIT, MAILSMSI, MAILMAIN, MAILCMD1, MAILSMSD, MAILCMD2,
MONWTR, LCLMOD.

@ux<Function>

@begin(enumerate)
Write startup message.

Variable GETMAIN for maximum available storage.

Calls LCLMOD routine if one was linked in.@index(LCLMOD)

Issue CP diagnose to get userid running under.

Call READINIT to read Mailer Profile.@index(READINIT)

Call SETINIT to set assorted MAILBLOK variables.@index(SETINIT)

Call MAILSMSI to set up SMSG interrupt hanlder.@index(MAILSMSI)

Check for any command flags and take the appropriate action: @tag<MAINLOOP>
@begin(Description)
MCMDRELD:@\@index(MCMDRELD)Reload by branching back to the top.

MCMDHALT:@\@index(MCMDHALT)Call MAILSMSD @index(MAILSMSD) to disable
SMSGs and exit.

MCMDCMD2:@\@index(MCMDCMD2)Call MAILCMD2 for more difficult command
processing.@index(MAILCMD2)
@end(description)

Call MAILMAIN to handle any reader files.@index(MAILMAIN)

Wait for a Console or Reader interrupt. 

For console interrupt,  call MAILCMD1 to parse the console issued
command. Go to @ref<MAINLOOP>.

For reader interrupt,  go to @ref<MAINLOOP>.
@end(enumerate)

@subsection(READINIT - Read Mailer Profile)
@index(READINIT)
@ux<Called by> MAILER.@index(MAILER)

@ux<Calls> UNIXSCAN, TBADD, TBLUK, MONWTR.

@ux<Mailer Profile format>

@index(Mailer Profile)
The Mailer Profile is a CMS file that has no restriction on
RECFM or LRECL.  Data within the file is composed of keyword
definitions and table definitions.  Blanks lines are ignored.
Comments are indicated by a semicolon and may appear anywhere
on a line.  Everything from the semicolon to the end of the
line is ignored.  Alphabetic case is ignored and values in
keyword assignments are converted to upper case.

Keywords are defined by a keyword followed by the symbol "=" followed
by a single character string. Blanks are considered a delimiter.
Tables are defined by the table name followed immediately by a colon.
The table name must be one of "INCOMING", "OUTGOING", or "SPECIAL".
A table end is marked by the keyword "END" followed by the table
name.@index(Incoming table)@index(Outgoing table)
@index(Special table)

@ux<Function>
@begin(enumerate)
Find Mailer Profile via FSSTATE using MAILRTFN as the source of
the file name.@index(MAILRTFN)

Use the LRECL from FSSTATE to allocate a single record input
buffer at the top of the stack.  Open the file.

(Pass 1) Call the PASS subroutine with the addresses of a keyword
counting and table entry counting routine as parameters.  These
routines count the number of entries in a given table and the number
of keyword  assignments seen.

Allocate storage from the stack for the 4 tables (MAILxTAB) using
the entry counts from Pass 1.

(Pass 2) Call the PASS subroutine with the addresses of a keyword
entry and table entry creation routine as parameters.  These routines
build the table entries and add them to the tables via TBADD.

Close the input file and free the input buffer from the stack top.
@end(enumerate)

@subsection(SETINIT - Set initial values)
@index(SETINIT)
@ux<Called by> MAILER.

@ux<Calls> TBLUK, LOAD, MONWTR.

@ux<Function>
SETINIT scans the data read by READINIT and sets MAILBLOK variables
from it.  It also causes the user exits named in the tables
to be dynamically loaded.
@begin(enumerate)
@index(MAILPTAB)
The MAILPTAB (parms table) is scanned for @i<Mailer> keywords.  These
keywords are:
@begin(description)
ThisNode@\RSCS node name of this system.@index(ThisNode parameter)

RSCSname@\Userid of RSCS server.@index(RSCSname parameter)

MaintUser@\Userid of maintainer.@index(MaintUser parameter)

MaintNode@\Node name of maintainer.@index(MaintNode parmeter)

Allow-Unknown-Nodes@\Value for flag MOPTAUN in MAILOPTS. Is either
"TRUE" or "FALSE".@index(Allow-Unknown-Nodes parameter)

Supply-Default-Fields@\Value for flag MOPTDEF in MAILOPTS.@index(
Supply-Default-Fields parameter)
@end(description)
The keywords fall into two categories: 8 character values to be
filled in and bits to be turned on or off.  These are encoded
in the tables PTABARR and PBITARR and may be easily expanded.@index(PBITARR)
@index(PTABARR)

The MAILRTAB table is processed by the LOADRTAB subroutine.@Index(MAILRTAB)
@index(LOADRTAB)
Correct values are checked and the named user exits are dynamically loaded
by the LOAD routine.

LOADRTAB is called for the MAILSTAB table.@index(MAILSTAB)

Similarly,  the MAILVTAB entries are checked and the validation user
exits are loaded.@index(MAILVTAB)
@end(enumerate)


@subsection(MAILMAIN - Main reader file processing routine)

@index(MAILMAIN)
@Ux<Called by> MAILER.

@ux<Calls> GETSPOOL,  GETHDR,  HDRSCAN,  VALIDATE,  BUILDLST,  SENDLST,
MAILRET.

@ux<Function>
@begin(Enumerate)
Call GETSPOOL@index(GETSPOOL) to get the spool file information. If the file is
not valid for some reason,  go to step @ref(RTS). If the reader
is empty, return. @tag(CHKRDR)

Call GETHDR@index(GETHDR) to read in the message header.  An erroneous header
results in a transfer to @ref(RTS).

Call HDRSCAN@index(HDRSCAN) to parse the header fields.  An error results in
step @ref(RTS).

Call VALIDATE@index(VALIDATE) to check the semantics of the syntactically correct
header.  Invalid semantics results in step @ref(RTS).

Call BUILDLST@index(BUILDLST) to build a list of recipients.  

Call SENDLST@index(SENDLST) to actually send mail to the recipients on the
list built.

Purge the reader file and go back to @ref(CHKRDR) to check for
any more files.

(Return to Sender) @tag(RTS) Call MAILRET@Index(MAILRET) to return the erroneous
file to it's sender if possible.
@end(Enumerate)

@subsection(GETSPOOL - Get spool file info)

@index(GETSPOOL)
@ux<Called by> MAILMAIN.@index(MAILMAIN)

@ux<Calls> MONWTR.

@ux<Function>
@begin(enumerate)
Issue CP diagnose x'14' subcode x'FFF' to get the next SFBLOK. @tag(GETSFB)
If the reader is empty, return with a return code to indicate that.

If the SFBLOK is marked in use, go to @ref(GETSFB) to try for the next file.
@index(SFBLOK)

Convert SFBFILID to character into MAILFTXT for later use.@index(SFBFILID)
@index(MAILFTXT)

Check SFBORIG to see it the file is from a local user or RSCS.@index(SFBORIG)

If local, set the MFLG1LCL and copy SFBORIG to MAILUSER and CPUSTR to MAILNODE.
@index(MFLG1LCL)@index(MAILUSER)@index(CPUSTR)@index(MAILNODE)

If from RSCS,  turn off MFLG1LCL and obtain MAILUSER and MAILNODE from
the tag text using the RSCSTAG dsect@Index(RSCS tag) in MAILER MACLIB. 
If the username is blank (from a locally attached RJE) set MAILUSER to 
"SYSTEM".
@end(enumerate)

@subsection(GETHDR - Read spool file header)
@index(GETHDR)
@ux<Called by> MAILMAIN

@ux<Calls> MONWTR

@ux<Function>

Reads cards in via RDCARD macro@index(RDCARD) and puts them in storage
allocated on the stack.  Each card read has trailing blanks removed
and a x'15' character added after it.  Cards are read until a blank
card or EOF is encountered.  The address of the stack storage used
for the header is put in the MAILHBUF pointer @index(MAILHBUF).
The MAILHREC counter@index(MAILHREC) contains the number of cards
read and the MAILHLIN counter@index(MAILHLIN) contains the number of
x'15' (newline) characters in the header buffer.  This allows imbedding
multiple newlines in a single card.  The ":READ" card generated by
the CMS PUNCH command is tested for and ignored if present.

@subsection(HDRSCAN - Scan message header)
@ux<Called by> MAILMAIN.@index(MAILMAIN)

@ux<Calls> TBADD, TBLUK, APARSE, DPARSE, DEFPARS, DESTPARS, MONWTR.

@ux<Function>

HDRSCAN parses the message header pointed to by MAILHBUF.@index(MAILHBUF)
Each header field name (i.e. "from", "subject") has a parser associated
with it in the PARSERS @index(PARSERS) table.  New field parsers are
easily added into this table. A default parser,  DEFPARS, @index(DEFPARS)
is called for field names that don't appear in the table.

@begin(enumerate)
Allocate the field name TBLUK table,  MAILKTAB, @index(MAILKTAB) from
the stack.

Scan the header buffer for a keyword.  This is defined as starting
at the left margin and delimitted by a colon.@tag(NEXTFLD)

Once a keyword is found,  scan until the beginning of the next keyword
is found to get the start and end addresses of the header field.

Fill in a FIELDD@index(FIELDD) with FISTR set to point just after the
colon and FIEND to point just prior to the next keyword (or buffer end).

Allocate a TOKEND for the field name and put its address if the FITOK
field.@index(TOKEND)

Chain the FIELDD after the previous FIELDD with the anchor of the
list at MAILFLD@index(MAILFLD).  This list can be used later to
access the fields in order of appearance in the header.

Call TBADD to add the field name to MAILKTAB.  The field may already
have been added before.

If the field was already there, then this is defined as an "extension
field".  That is, another occurence of the same field name elsewhere
in the header.  This is legal syntax and semantically can be
considered as a concatenation of the data to the previous data for the
field.  The FIEXTN pointer in the previous FIELDD for the field is set
to point to the new FIELDD.  This allows later access to all FIELDDs
for the same field name in the order of appearance.

If the field was not already there,  then it's keyword has been
added to the table.  Subsequent routines may easily find the
FIELDD for the required field by calling TBLUK with the field name required.

Go back to @ref(NEXTFLD) until all field names have been processed.

Once all the field names have been picked up and FIELDDs built,  a second
pass is taken through the MAILKTAB table.  Call TBLUK with the
PARSERS table for the next MAILKTAB entry.@tag(NEXTFLD2)

If the entry is found,  the return value is the address of the
parser to call.

If the entry is not found,  use the DEFPARS parser.

Call this parser with the FIELDD as argument.
The parser is expected to add TOKENDs to the FITOK list and 
may optionally use the FIPARS pointer for a data representation
particular to the field type being parsed. 

On an error return code from the parser,  return after issuing
an error message and setting an error return code.

Continue until all fields have been parsed. Go to @ref(NEXTFLD2).
@end(enumerate)


@subsection(APARSE, ADPARSE - Parse address fields)
@index(APARSE)@index(ADPARSE)
@ux<Called by> HDRSCAN

@ux<Calls> TOKENIZR

@ux<Function>

APARSE is called by HDRSCAN with a FIELDD as a parameter.  It 
uses the FIELDD to build the parameter list for the actual
address parser, ADPARSE.  ADPARSE takes as arguments the start and
end address of the string to be parsed, a stack pointer, and
pointers to be filled in for the token list and parse list.
These output fields are filled in FITOK and FIPARS respectively
by APARSE.

@begin(enumerate)
Call TOKENIZR@index(TOKENIZR) to convert the input string into
a linked list of TOKENDs allocated from the stack.  The entire
input string is tokenized prior to parsing.

Chain through the token list.  

Allocate an ADADDR @index(ADADDR) record and chain it from the
previous one via the ADANEXT pointer.  The list anchor is 
later returned and plugged into the FIPARS of the FIELDD.

For each user name token parsed,  allocate an ADNODE@index(ADNODE) and set the
ADNDOWN pointer to the TOKEND for the user name atom.  If a user name
contains multiple atoms,  then a list of ADNODEs is produced, with each
pointing to the next via the ADNNEXT pointer.  The number of ADNODEs
in this list is put in the ADAUCNT field of the ADADDR record and the
address of the first ADNODE in the list is put in the ADAULST pointer.

For each host name token parsed, allocate an ADNODE as in the previous
step.  Each ADNODE here specifies the next host in a path (as in
@@HOSTA@@HOSTB@@HOSTC). The number of host name ADNODEs is put in
the ADAHCNT field of the ADADDR and the ADAHLST field gets the
address of the first ADNODE.
@end(enumerate)

This module is currently being rewritten for the next release of
@i<Mailer>.  It will output a very similar if not identical representation
but will operate more elegantly than the current horrendous code
and will support more of the RFC733 syntax.  

@subsection(DEFPARS, DESTPARS, - Other field parsers)
@index(DEFPARS)@index(DESTPARS)

Other less complicated parsers are provided for non-address message
header fields.  The functions of these parsers are:
@begin(description)
DEFPARS@\The default parser creates one TOKEND@index(TOKEND) for each
line of text in the field.  Generally, this is only one but may be
more if the header field contains multiple lines.  The token type
is set to quoted-string.  FIPARS is not used.

DESTPARS@\This parser is for the "*DESTINATION:" field used by the
@i<Mailer> gateway function.  It creates one atom TOKEND for each
hostname in a list seperate by commas or blanks.  FIPARS is not
used.
@end(description)

@subsection(VALIDATE - Check validity of message)
@index(VALIDATE)
@ux<Called by> MAILMAIN.

@ux<Calls> TBLUK, MONWTR.

@ux<Function>

The required message fields are checked for as well as special
funny cases such as a file that @i<Mailer> has sent to itself
for the purposes of reporting an error or a file rejected by
RSCS because of an invalid tag.

@begin(enumerate)
Check if the file was generated by @i<Mailer>. Test MFLG1LCL
@index(MFLG1LCL) to see if the file is locally generated
and compare MAILUSER and MAILVMID@index(MAILUSER)@index(MAILVMID)
for the same userid.

If the previous step is satisfied,  check the tag text for the
special tag "--- returned mail file ---" which indicates it was
generated by the MAILRET@index(MAILRET) routine.  If so,  go on
to step @ref(NOTSPCL) for standard processing.  If not,  the
file was probably rejected by RSCS.  Write an error message
and set the MFLG1REJ flag@index(MFLG1REJ) to indicate this case.

Call TBLUK with the MAILKTAB@index(MAILKTAB) table
for the string "Date". @tag(NOTSPCL)

If "Date" is not found and MOPTDEF@index(MOPTDEF) is off,  then
the file is invalid.  Write an error message and return.  If
MOPTDEF is on,  fill in a default "Date" field (NYI).

Call TBLUK to check for existence of "From" field.  

If found,  test the FIELDD returned to see that the field has
only one address and not a list.  If this is true, go on the
@ref(CHKSNDR).

Call TBLUK to check for existence of "Sender" field.  Test
as in the previous step.  If this succeeds, go on to @ref(CHKSNDR)

Test the MOPTDEF flag and supply the default sender if the file
is from a local user only (MFLG1LCL on). (NYI)

Test the MFLG1REJ@index(MFLG1REJ) flag and return if it is set.@tag(CHKSNDR)

Test the MFLG1LCL flag and if off (file from network), go to step
@ref(NETWORK).

(File from a Local sender) Look at the sender field and compare the
sending userid to MAILUSER to see if the sender names match.  

Look at the sender field host name by calling TBLUK with the MAILRTAB
@index(MAILRTAB) table to see if the specified host name is an alias
for the local system. (compare CPUSTR@index(CPUSTR) and RTROUTE
of the ROUTD @index(ROUTD) returned.)

If the above tests succeed,  return successfully.

(File from a network sender) Call TBLUK with the MAILVTAB
@index(MAILVTAB) table to see if there is a VALD for the named
sender node (MAILNODE) and userid (MAILUSER) @index(MAILNODE)
@index(MAILUSER).@index(VALD)@tag(NETWORK)

If one is found,  then check to see if a validation user exit
(address in VAADDR) was supplied.  If so call it.  If not,
return successfully.

If one is not found, check MOPTAUN@index(MOPTAUN) to see if unknown
nodes are allowed.  If off,  return failure.

If unknown nodes are allowed,  call TBLUK again to see if a default
validation exit was supplied.  Call it if found, else return.
@end(enumerate)

@subsection(BUILDLST - Build list of recipients)
@index(BUILDLST)
@ux<Called by> MAILMAIN

@ux<Calls> TBLUK, MONWTR.

@ux<Function>

BUILDLST generates a list of ACTADR blocks for each recipient 
that @i<Mailer> is a server for.  This list is pointed to by
MAILCHN@index(MAILCHN) in MAILBLOK.
@begin(enumerate)
Call TBLUK with MAILKTAB@index(MAILKTAB) for each address field
("To", "CC", "BCC") and retain pointers to the FIELDDs for the
fields that are found.

For each ADADDR list,  the BLDACT routine is called.

BLDACT: Get next ADADDR record.@tag(NXTADA)@index(ADADDR)

Obtain the last host name TOKEND@index(TOKEND) by following the list
of tokens from ADAHLST to the end.

If MFLGDEST@index(MFLGDEST) is on,  indicating "*DESTINATION" gateway
processing,  search the destination list to see if the host name is
on it.  If not, ignore this ADADDR and  go to step @ref(NXTADA).

Call TBLUK with the MAILRTAB@index(MAILRTAB) table to find the entry
for the named host.

If not found, see if the Allow-Unknown-Nodes option was specified
(MOPTAUN on)@index(MOPTAUN) and if so,  search MAILRTAB for a
ROUTD for "*DEFAULT*". If found,  continue with the following steps,
otherwise ignore this ADADDR and go to step @ref(NXTADA).

If found, check if one of the following is true:
@begin(enumerate)
MFLG1LCL is on -- File is from a local user.

MFLG1GTW is on -- File is from a gateway server.

MFLGDEST is on -- Usual logic is being overridden by the *DESTINATION list.

CPUSTR@index(CPUSTR) equal to RTROUTE field of ROUTD@index(ROUTD) --
This @i<Mailer> is the server for files for the host.
@end(enumerate)

If the above step is true,  then add the ADADDR and ROUTD onto the
ACTADR chain.

If the step was false,  then ignore this address and go to step @ref(NXTADA).

@end(enumerate)


@subsection(SENDLST - Send mail using list of recipients)
@index(SENDLST)
@ux<Called by> MAILMAIN.

@ux<Calls> MONWTR,  outgoing user exits.

@ux<Function>

@begin(enumerate)
Get the next ACTADR@index(ACTADR) record from the chain pointed to by
MAILCHN@index(MAILCHN).@tag(SND1)

Get the ROUTD@index(ROUTD) from the ACTRT pointer in the ACTADR.

If the RTADDR is non-zero,  then call the user exit (whose name is
in RTPROG and loaded address in RTADDR).

If the user exit return code is non-zero,  write a message and loop
back to @ref(SND1) for the next ACTADR.

Once all ACTADRs have been processed,  return failure if any user exit
had a non-zero return code.  Otherwise,  return success.
@end(enumerate)

@subsection(MAILRET - Return to sender)
@index(MAILRET)
@ux<Called by> MAILMAIN.

@ux<Calls> MONWTR, ARPADATE, PUNHDR, PUNTXT, PUNCLS.

@ux<Function>

@begin(Enumerate)

(File from local user) If the file is from a local user (MFLG1LCL
true)@index(MFLG1LCL) and was not rejected by RSCS (MFLG1REJ false)
@index(MFLG1REJ) then check to make sure the mail sender
(MAILUSER)@index(MAILUSER) is not the same as the @i<Mailer> userid
(MAILVMID)@index(MAILVMID).  If these are the same, an error loop has
been detected.

If an error loop was detected,  then check to make sure that the
maintainer,  MAINTID, @index(MAINTID) is not the same as the @i<Mailer>
id.  If so,  just return after writing an error message.  If
they are different, go to step @ref(MRET) to return the file.

(File from network or rejected by RSCS) If the file was rejected by
RSCS (MFLG1REJ true) or is from the network (MFLG1LCL false) then
try to extract the userid that caused the error from the
"from" or "sender" field.  If valid,  this field is pointed to
by MAILSFLD@index(MAILSFLD).  This field is zero if the sender
was not successfully parsed.

If MAILSFLD  is zero,  then try to send  the file to the maintainer
with the appropriate loop checking done as described above.

Each entry of the ADADDR and ADNODE pointed to by MAILSFLD should
be checked carefully even though it was already parsed.  @b<This
is the major source of bugs in @i{Mailer}> and will lead to infinite
looping if care is not taken.

Given the sender field is correct,  go to step @ref(MRET) to 
return the file.  

Spool the punch to self (i.e. "SP PUN TO *") and set the punch
tag to  "--- returned mail file ---" for communication with
the VALIDATE@index(VALIDATE) routine.  @tag(MRET)

Punch a message header that explains an error has occured.

Punch the error log file (named in MAILULFN)@index(MAILULFN) if it
exists.  This file contains the accumulated error messages.

If MFLG1HDS is true@index(MAILHDS), indicating the header was properly
scanned,  then call PUNHDR,  PUNTXT,  and PUNCLS to punch the message
header,  punch the message text,  and close the punch.

If the header was not scanned,  just loop with RDCARDs and PUNCHCs
to copy the reader file to the punch file.
@end(Enumerate)

A lot of bugs seem to pop up in this routine and in the punching
exits it calls.  A lot of care must be taken to insure that
assumptions that can normally be made about a file   are not made
here since the file wouldn't be erroneous in most cases if those
assumptions were true.

@subsection(MAILSMSI, MAILSMSG, MAILSMSD - SMSG routines)
@index(MAILSMSI)@index(MAILSMSG)@index(MAILSMSD)

MAILSMSI initializes SMSG processing by doing a VMCF SENDX authorize
and a HNDEXT SET with MAILSMSG as the external interrupt handler.
It also turns on bit 0 of control register 0.

MAILSMSD disables SMSG processing by doing a VMCF unauthorize and
a HNDEXT CLR.

MAILSMSG is entered on an external interrupt.  It tests the interrupt
code for X'4001',  the VMCF function code for VMCPSENX,  and the
VMCMUSER for "SYSTEM".  Any other interrupts are ignored as well
as SMSGs with a zero data length.  It then calls MAILCMD1 to process
the command received.

@subsection(MAILCMD1 - 1st level command processing)
@index(MAILCMD1)
@ux<Called by> MAILER,  MAILSMSG.

@ux<Calls> UNIXSCAN, TBLUK.

@ux<Parameters> 

@begin(description)
SENDER@\Userid of sender (for smsgs) or * (for others).

STRING@\Command string.

CODE@\0 for command from console or 1 for command from SMSG.
@end(description)

@ux<Function>

This reentrant routine may be entered either by a call from
@index<MAILSMSG> MAILSMSG or from MAILER.@index(MAILER) In the former
case, external interrupts are disabled.  MAILCMD1 only performs trivial
commands such as @i<Mailer> internal commands and CP commands.  More
complex commands are saved for MAILCMD2 @index<MAILCMD2>.  Command
functions:
@begin(description) 
? or HELP@\Send a short message describing commands.

ACTIVATE@\Turn off the MSTQSC @index<MSTQSC> flag.

CHECK@\(NYI)

CMS@\Allocate a COMMAND @index<COMMAND> block in DMSFREE storage.  Copy in the
command data and other information and add it onto the the command
list pointed to by MAILCMDL @index<MAILCMDL>.  If called from the console,  there
is a possibility of being reentered via SMSG,  so disable external
interrupts whenever MAILCMDL is modified.

CP@\Issue the CP command via Diagnose 8 and trap the reply in a buffer.
Return this buffer to the command issuer.

FORWARD@\(NYI)

QUIESCE@\Set MSTQSC@index(MSTQSC) to true.  

RELOAD@\Set MCMDRELD@index<MCMDRELD> true.

SHOW@\Options are:
@begin(description)
VERSION@\Reply with the @i<Mailer> version number and last assembly
date.

STATUS@\Reply with some counters and interpretation of the MAILSTAT @index<MAILSTAT>
status flags.

OUTGOING@\Show the ROUTD contents for the named host.  "SHOW OUTGOING *"
will list all ROUTDs.

INCOMING@\Show the VALD contents for the named node and userid pair.
"SHOW INCOMING *" will list all VALDs.

SPECIAL@\Same as SHOW OUTGOING except use the MAILSTAB@index<MAILSTAB> instead
of MAILRTAB@index<MAILRTAB>.
@end(description)


SMSG@\Set a flag indicating the reply should be SMSG instead of MSGNOH
or MSG.  Go back and try the next token as the command name.

STOP@\Set MCMDHALT@index<MCMDHALT> true.
@end(description)

Authorization is checked for all commands and falls into two classes:
General and Privileged.  General commands may be issued via SMSG by
any user.  Privileged commands are issued via SMSG from the
maintainer or from the console.

@subsection(MAILCMD2 - 2nd level command processing)
@index<MAILCMD2>
@ux<Called by> MAILER.

@ux<Function>

@begin(enumerate)
Disable external interrupts.

Copy MAILCMDL@index<MAILCMDL> pointer and clear it.

Enable external interrupts.

Get next COMMAND@index<COMMAND>.

Check code for CMD202 (the only type currently recognized).

Issue ATTN LIFO via SVC 202 to stack the command and then
issue SUBSET (RETURN to execute the command.

Release the DMSFREE storage and do the next COMMAND.
@end(enumerate)

@subsection(PUNHDR, PUNTXT, PUNCLS - Output punching routines)
@index(PUNHDR)@index(PUNTXT)@index(PUNCLS)
@ux<Called by> User outgoing exits.

@ux<Function>

These routines punch the output mail file in a standard way.
It is not possible to simply copy the input file to the output file
since some records in the input file should be discarded (such
as :READ card, inter-mailer communication information) or the
header may have been edited.  

PUNHDR uses the FIELDD@index(FIELDD) list anchored at MAILFLD@index(MAILFLD)
to output the header.  If MFLG1EDI @index(MFLG1EDI) is off, indicating
that the header was not edited,  then each FIELDD is used to access the
data in MAILHBUF@index(MAILHBUF).  Only the records pointed to by FISTR and
FIEND for FIELDDs that have the FIINVFLG off are punched.  This allows an
easy way to prevent output of certain input fields.

PUNTXT simply skips the first MAILHREC@index(MAILHREC) records in the
input file and then copies the rest to the output file.

PUNCLS closes the punch and sets the filename to be the user name from
MAILSFLD@index(MAILSFLD).  The filetype is set to "MAIL",  and the
distribution code to the host name from MAILSFLD.

@subsection(MONWTR, USERLOG, WRITELOG, SNDRMSG - Message routines)
@index(MONWTR)
MONWTR is the centralized message writer routine.  All @i<Mailer>
message skeletons are in the MSGTEXT CSECT.  Messages are of the
standard error message form.  The first three letters of the
message number is "MAI".  The second three indicate the module name.
The letter after the three digit number indicates how the message
is to be handled.  All messages are written to the console by calling
WRITELOG.  They may also be written to the user error log file and
sent as network messages.  The following letters have a special meaning:
@begin(description)
U@\Write to the user log file (MAILULFN)@index(MAILULFN) by calling
USERLOG.

X@\Send the message to the file sender (from MAILSFLD) @index(MAILSFLD)
by calling SNDRMSG  which issues an RSCS MSG command.
@end(description)

MONWTR is called by the WTR or LOG macros.  These macros differ only in
that LOG requires MAILBLOK addressability to get the MONWTR address.
This is the one needed for user exits.  The macro format is described
in figure @ref(WTRFIG).

@begin(figure)
@caption(LOG macro format)
@tag(WTRFIG)
@begin(example)

LOG @i<mmmnnn>[,(@i<type,var,type,var>,...)]

@end(example)

Where @i<mmm> is the three letter module identifier, @i<nnn> the three digit 
message number, @i<type> the data type, and @i<var> any A-type address 
constant or a register number coded within parentheses.

@i<types> are:
@begin(description)
D or DA@\Decimal value or address of fullword.

X or XA@\Hexadecimal value or address of hexadecimal bytes (length determined
by message skeleton).

B or BA@\Bit string value or address of bit string (length determined by 
message skeleton).

C or CA@\Character string value or address of character string (length
determined by message skeleton).
@end(description)
@end(figure)

@subsection(TBLUK, TBADD - General table management routines)
@index(TBLUK)

TBLUK is based on a DEC TOPS-20 Monitor Call of the same name.
TBLUK performs table lookups and TBADD, additions.

A TBLUK table is a standard format table of doublewords. The left
word points to a string descriptor which contains flags and the
keyword string.  The right word is available to the user.  This
word is normally called the dispatch value.  In @i<Mailer>,  this
word is used in various places to contain record addresses (i.e.
ROUTD, VALD),  branch addresses (command names table in MAILCMD1)
and subroutine addresses (PARSERS table in HDRSCAN).

A TBLUK table may easily be build by using the TBSTART, T, and TBEND
macros.  A sample TBLUK table defined with these macros follows.
Note that the keywords must be specified in lexicographic order.

@begin(example,group)
CMDTABLE TBSTART ,		START OF COMMAND TABLE
	 T 'ADD',0
	 T 'DIVIDE'
 	 T 'MULTIPLY'
	 T 'SUBTRACT'
	 TBEND ,
@end(example)

For T macro entries that do not supply a dispatch value,  a symbol
constructed of "$" followed by the first 7 letters of the keyword.
For example, the dispatch values for the table above are: 0,
$DIVIDE, $MULTIPL, $SUBTRAC.

@ux<TBLUK parameters>

TBLUK is called by coding CALL TBLUK,(table,string,length,work)
where @i<table> is the table address, @i<string> is the test string
address, @i<length> is the string length,  and @i<work> is the address
of a work buffer that has a maximum length of the length of the longest
possible test string and the longest possible table keyword with a
maximum of 512.

@ux<TBLUK return values>

@begin(description)
Reg 0@\Address of matched table entry.

Reg 1@\Dispatch value.

Reg 15@\0 for exact match.  4 for unambiguous substring.  8 for ambiguous
substring.  12 for no match.
@end(description)

TBLUK entries are mapped by the TBENTRY macro.  TBLUK keyword strings
are mapped by the TBS macro.  The TBTAB macro maps a TBLUK table header.
TBLUK is reentrant.

TBADD adds a new entry to a TBLUK table.

@subsection(LOAD - dynamic loader)
@index(LOAD)
@ux<Called by> SETINIT.

@u<Function>

LOAD is similar in concept to the OS LOAD macro.  It has the added
advantages of being able to decide where a text is loaded and to
prevent abends on trivial errors.  It is called with a stack pointer
and routine name.  A "LOAD name (ORIGIN address NOMAP" command is
built and issued via SVC 202.  The LOCCNT@index(LOCCNT) field
in NUCON is checked to see how much space the loaded routine required.
This amount is used to update the stack pointer.  It is possible
for LOAD to clobber storage that follows the stack if the
routine being loaded is too large.  This is highly unlikely however.

@section(User exits)

This section should act as a guide to coding @i<Mailer> user exits.
User exits are provided at the following points:
@begin(itemize)
As the LCLMOD exit called prior to READINIT in MAILER.

A validation exit called in VALIDATE.

An outgoing punching exit called in SENDLST.
@end(itemize)


@subsection(Calling conventions)

All @i<Mailer> routines use standard OS subroutine linkage:
@begin(description)
R1@\Address of parameter list.

R13@\Caller's register save area.

R14@\Caller's return address.

R15@\Entry point address.
@end(description)

These exits are loaded dynamically and therefore may not reference
VCONS without the fear of the wonderful CMS loader blowing @i<Mailer>
out of the water.  If a multiple module exit is required,  do the
same thing that RSCS does:  use the PRELOAD module supplied with
your RSCS system as per the instructions in the RSCS documentation.

@subsection(LCLMOD - Local Modifications hook)
@index(LCLMOD)

@ux<Called via>

@begin(example)
CALL LCLMOD,(MAILBLOK)
@end(example)

This routine may be supplied by an installation to modify fields in
the MAILBLOK prior to calling READINIT.@index(READINIT) This is mostly
useful in a case where the name of the Mailer Profile is not known
until runtime (i.e. on a site with 2 cpu's sharing dasd). In this
case, LCLMOD can check the processor serial number (via STIDP
instruction or CP QUERY CPUID) and set the appropriate file name in
MAILBLOK accordingly.

@subsection(VALD exit)

@ux<Called via>

@begin(example)
CALL exit,(MAILBLOK)
@end(example)

This exit is called after the file has been parsed.  It is free to check
anything it wants to look at in MAILBLOK.  A non-zero return code tells
@i<Mailer> to return the file to its sender.

This exit should zero the MAILSFLD pointer if it determines that the
sender field is bogus and could cause problems when trying to return
the mail.  

By using a keyword such as "PASSWORD:" and the FIINVFLG flag in the
FIELDD@index(FIELDD) for it,  a simple method of password-protecting
a file over a non-secure HASP link has been done in the DECVER exit
at CUCCA.  The keyword may be found by calling TBLUK@index(TBLUK)
with the MAILKTAB@index(MAILKTAB) table.  Use of FIINVFLG on the 
FIELDD prevents the password from being copied to the output file.

An example of a simple password-checking VALD exit follows:
@begin(EXAMPLE,GROUP)
VALCHK  CSECT ,
	STM   14,12,12(13)
	LR    12,15		ADDRESSABILITY ETC.
	USING VALCHK,15
	ST    13,VALSAVE+4
	LA    13,VALSAVE
	L     11,0(1)		GET MAILBLOK BASE
	USING MAILBLOK,11
	L     5,MAILKTAB	GET KEYWORD TABLE
	L     15,ATBLUK		ADDRESS OF TBLUK
	CALL  (15),((5),KEY,L'KEY,WORK)
	LTR   15,15		FIND IT?
	BNZ   NOKEY		SORRY...
	LR    6,1		GET BETTER HANDLE ON THE FIELDD
	USING FIELDD,6		ADDRESS IT
	OI    FIFLG,FIINVFLG	MAKE THIS FIELD "INVISIBLE"
	L     1,FITOK 		POINT TO THE "PASSWORD:" TOKEN
	ICM   1,B'0111',TOKNEXT GET NEXT TOKEN
	BZ    NOKEY		"PASSWORD:" BUT NOT PASSWORD!
	USING TOKEND,1		ADDRESS THE TOKEN
	CLI   TOKLEN,L'PASS 	SAME LENGTH AS PASSWORD?
	BNE   NOKEY		SORRY...
	ICM   1,B'0111',TOKSTR  POINT TO STRING
	DROP  1
	CLC   PASS,0(1) 	COMPARE PASSWORDS
	BNE   NOKEY		SORRY....
	SR    15,15		YAY!
RET	EQU   *
	L     13,4(13)	
	L     14,12(13)
	LM    0,12,20(13)
	BR    14
NOKEY	EQU   *
	LA    15,4
	B     RET
KEY	DC    C'PASSWORD'
PASS	DC    C'SECRET-PASSWORD'
WORK	DS    64F
SAVE    DS    18F
	BLOCKS ,		MACRO TO GET ALL DSECTS
	MAILBLOK ,		...EXCEPT MAILBLOK
	END   VALCHK
@end(EXAMPLE)

@subsection(ROUTD exit)

@ux<Called via>

@begin(example)
CALL exit,(MAILBLOK,ACTADR)@index(ACTADR)
@end(example)

This exit is responsible for transmitting the mail file in the
reader to it's recipient.  It has access to the ROUTD and ADADDR
for the recipient as well as the routines PUNHDR, PUNTXT, and PUNCLS
pointed to by APUNHDR@index(PUNHDR), APUNTXT@index(PUNTXT) and APUNCLS
@index(PUNCLS) in MAILBLOK.  TBLUK is pointed to by ATBLUK.

The example above for the VALD exit shows all the basic information
necessary to code an outgoing exit.
